WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.357
The pretrained-model ecosystem is far too large and changes too quickly for memorizing model names to be a

00:00:07.357 --> 00:00:08.682
useful skill.

00:00:08.682 --> 00:00:17.577
Public hubs contain a huge number of repositories for base models, fine-tuned variants, quantized versions,

00:00:17.577 --> 00:00:23.207
adapters, embedding models, rerankers, multimodal systems, and many other specialized checkpoints.

00:00:23.207 --> 00:00:29.987
The exact count changes constantly as models are uploaded, updated, or removed.

00:00:29.987 --> 00:00:32.337
A better skill is filtering.

00:00:32.337 --> 00:00:35.627
Start by defining the task.

00:00:35.627 --> 00:00:42.302
Then narrow by architecture, supported language, domain, model size, license, hardware requirements,

00:00:42.302 --> 00:00:43.907
and evaluation evidence.

00:00:43.907 --> 00:00:50.927
For semantic search, do not browse general chat models first; look for embedding models.

00:00:50.927 --> 00:00:55.322
For document reranking, look for rerankers.

00:00:55.322 --> 00:01:02.692
For sentiment classification, look for a model already fine-tuned for that task or plan to fine-tune an

00:01:02.692 --> 00:01:08.147
encoder yourself. Model hubs are catalogs, not recommendation engines.

00:01:08.147 --> 00:01:14.640
The responsibility for matching a checkpoint to your problem remains with you.
